**Read:** [Materiais de Construção Mecânica — Zolin](../sources/Materiais-de-Construcao-Mecanica-Zolin.lingua#r_1KS5EGZJ8PBFDART80K21J1309), properties; [Materials structure and observation — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Materials-Structure-and-Observation-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_57315283EDNWKYC1PQE23G2AKF), mechanical properties and anisotropy. The linked mechanical-engineering formation supplies full load/deformation derivations.

Under a simple uniform axial elastic model, a bar's extension is FL/(EA). Its axial stiffness EA/L changes with area and length even if the material modulus E does not. A larger test force may indicate a larger specimen rather than a stronger material. A property inferred from a test inherits assumptions about geometry, loading and measurement.

For F=1000 N, L=0.2 m, A=100 mm² and E=200 GPa, the predicted extension is 10 μm. Doubling area halves extension in this model. Neither calculation establishes yielding, fracture, buckling, creep or the behavior of a joint.

**Work:** Compare two geometries made from one material and two materials in the same geometry. Identify the normalization needed for a meaningful comparison. Describe a situation where the uniform-bar model fails. Connect a measured displacement to the specimen and fixture compliances before treating it as a material property.

**Return:** [Materials engineering](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
